From: degas device tree
Subject: [PATCH] BatteryStats: ignore an implausible charge time from the health HAL

computeChargeTimeRemaining() returns whatever the health HAL reports as
chargeTimeToFullNowSeconds the moment it is not negative, and only falls back to
the estimate measured from the charge steps when the HAL declines to answer.
That trusts a number the HAL does not compute: MediaTeks health service passes
/sys/class/power_supply/battery/time_to_full_now straight through.

On degas (Xiaomi 14T, mt6897) that node is wrong by two and a half orders of
magnitude. Measured on hardware while charging at 67 W: the battery takes
2030 mA (current_now -2031000, charge_counter climbing 2057 mA, bms/av_current
-2033) and has 3.6 Ah to go, i.e. under two hours - and the node reports
2934120 seconds, falling by 35400 seconds per percent, which is the arithmetic
of a 4.4 mA charge. Settings duly tells the user the phone will be full in
36 days.

The step tracker in this very class had the right answer all along: 74 s per
percent, about 1 h 40 min from 18 %. So cap what the HAL is allowed to claim and
let the measured estimate take over past that.

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/power/stats/BatteryStatsImpl.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/power/stats/BatteryStatsImpl.java
index 574abe634823..dfd34d8b0647 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/power/stats/BatteryStatsImpl.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/power/stats/BatteryStatsImpl.java
@@ -13995,13 +13995,26 @@ public class BatteryStatsImpl extends BatteryStats {
         return mDailyDischargeStepTracker;
     }
 
+    /**
+     * An upper bound on a charge time estimate that is worth believing. The health
+     * HAL hands this figure straight through from the charger driver, and a driver
+     * that gets it wrong is not rare: on some MediaTek gauges
+     * /sys/class/power_supply/battery/time_to_full_now is computed from a current
+     * of a few milliamps while the battery is taking two amps, which reports a
+     * phone on a 67 W charger as "full in 36 days". Past this limit the estimate
+     * measured from the charge steps below is used instead - it is derived from
+     * levels this class saw itself, so it cannot be off by orders of magnitude.
+     */
+    private static final long MAX_PLAUSIBLE_CHARGE_TIME_SECONDS = 12 * 60 * 60;
+
     @Override
     public long computeChargeTimeRemaining(long curTime) {
         if (mOnBattery) {
             // Not yet working.
             return -1;
         }
-        if (mBatteryTimeToFullSeconds >= 0) {
+        if (mBatteryTimeToFullSeconds >= 0
+                && mBatteryTimeToFullSeconds <= MAX_PLAUSIBLE_CHARGE_TIME_SECONDS) {
             return mBatteryTimeToFullSeconds * (1000 * 1000); // s to us
         }
         // Else use algorithmic approach
